Fillets: a contour never takes an edge of its own again, so that the walk
along a tangent chain ends (mitcad#434).

The case: a closed chain of edges whose edges turn at a vertex W, the faces
going on tangentially across it, and the contour started from an edge
selected for the turns (StopChainsAtTurns, patch 0180) while the edge
beyond W is not. PerformElement's downstream walk stops at W (StopsAtTurn:
a turn before an edge not selected). Its upstream walk from the contour's
start goes round the chain the other way and comes to W from the other
side, where the edge beyond is the downstream walk's last edge, selected:
StopsAtTurn lets it pass, and the walk went on over the contour's edges,
prepending them to the spine for ever. Nothing stops it: the downstream
walk ends where it comes back to its start vertex, the upstream walk has no
such check, and BRepFilletAPI_MakeFillet::Add has no progress range, so a
request to stop does not reach it either. In the `.f3d` import a definition
of a fillet with some of a closed chain's edges ran for hours.

- PerformElement: the edges of the contour so far are kept in a map, and
  neither walk adds an edge that is in it; the walk stops there as at an
  edge it does not take. Round such a chain the contour is every edge of
  the chain once, both its ends at W.
